{{SubstanceBox/Buspirone}}
'''Buspirone''' (trade name '''Buspar''' among others) is an [[Anxiety suppression|anxiolytic]] substance of the [[azapirone]] class. It is primarily used to treat [[Anxiety|anxiety disorders]], particularly generalized anxiety disorder (GAD.)<ref>[https://www.drugs.com/monograph/buspirone.html "Buspirone Hydrochloride Monograph for Professionals".] ''[https://drugs.com Drugs.com]''</ref> It is a [[serotonin]] 5-HT<sub>1A</sub> [[receptor]] [[Agonist|partial agonist]], increasing action at serotonin receptors in the brain.<ref>Loane C, Politis M (June 2012). "Buspirone: what is it all about?". ''Brain Research''. '''1461''': 111–118. https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.brainres.2012.04.032</ref> 

It is often prescribed as a safer alternative to [[benzodiazepines]] for anxiety as it does not activate [[GABA|GABA receptors]] and does not possess any addiction liability.<ref>Wilson TK, Tripp J (January 2018). [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531477/ "Buspirone".]</ref> At therapeutic doses, it has no immediate anxiolytic effects and hence has a delayed [[Onset|onset of action]]; its full clinical effectiveness may require 2–4 weeks to manifest itself.
